\f1\fs22 Hayley took the Rail home, and instantly regretted it. At this time of night on a Friday, the Airborne carriages ought to have been safe territory, but even they\'92d been invaded by opportunists: Cloudborne party girls left over from a rave, stray kids running wild in the aisles, and a couple of blokes with DIY tattoos who eyed everyone as if weighing up wallet-snatching potential.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 A sou-easter from the Tasman whipped turbid tendrils of fog out from the top of the Layer, the Rail plunging in and out of the soup. Even \'93premium\'94 aircon up top couldn\'92t scrub all the particulates; Hayley coughed as chemical grit caught in her throat, while the rest of the carriage rode in indifferent calm\'97napping, chewing on dubious convenience sushi, or serenely staring dead-eyed into nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As much to take her attention off the weather as anything else, Hayley thumbed through the photos of Halo she\'92d skimmed from Abe\'92s tablet. They were quick snaps: off-kilter framing, faces half-obscured, but still enough to chart patterns. Halo\'92s smile was pure confidence: urbane, pick-up-artist calm, the sort of man who looked most himself when the outcome was already rigged in his favour. Even when his expression was benign, his eyes watched the women: gently interested, just a touch predatory, always in control.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was the women, though\'85 The women leaned in toward Halo, their faces eager, eyes wide, hands sometimes pressed to his chest or hip. Hayley zoomed in and saw traces: dilated pupils, softly bitten lips, hair tucked back, cheeks flushed. When she refocused on Halo, his eyes seemed to glow: a strange, lilac shade, brighter than the rest of his face. Maybe a photo artefact, maybe not.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She scrolled back through the album, looking for more. Again and again, the eyes: sometimes lilac, sometimes grey, sometimes one of each, as if mid-transformation. It was as if Halo\'92s eyes were capable of changing moment by moment\'97pupil size fluctuating, even the length of his eyelashes shifting, though logically that was a trick of the light or lens. Hayley hovered on the odd split-second image with the mismatched eyes, wondering: Had Abe caught the beat where one eye changed before the other?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She knew what could cause that. The shock sent her gaze snapping up from the tablet to a commuter across the way, quietly watching her. \'93Empathy tech,\'94 Hayley muttered\'97not loud enough to be heard, just enough to put the idea out in the carriage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Hayley didn\'92t sleep. She rolled, thrashed, committed acts of pillow violence, strangled her sheets, accidentally kicked Chainsaw off the bed three times, and gave the ceiling her very best death-stare. Wired to snapping, she gave up and pleaded with her shower to deliver a stress-dissolving torrent of scalding water. Then, towelled and turbaned, she offered only a nod to her gown and slippers\'97they knew their jobs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 In the kitchen, she set the coffee to brew, grabbed her puter, and sank onto a stool at the island bench, ready to dig. She hit up an ID site, dragged in one of Abe\'92s images, and, after a moment, was served up a bland bureaucratic profile. \'93Haloren Smythe,\'94 the headline read, his photo beside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Occupation: Life Coach. Hayley sneered. Life coaches: only ever driven to coach the living daylights out of whoever paid their bill. No one Hayley had ever known had benefitted, though, to be fair, you could say the same for most of Hayley\'92s friends.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Give me everything on Haloren Smythe,\'94 she told her computer. It got to work, chugging, cogitating, squirting off what might have been digital steam.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While she waited, Hayley snatched the coffee pot from its screams, poured herself a mug, sniffed, winced. It tasted like piss. She dumped it, boiled the home defence kettle\'97ever wary of it trying to inject her with capsaicin\'97and brewed tea instead.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The tea tasted like a different sort of piss. Maybe infected with 
\f2\i E. Coli
\f1\i0  or klebsiella. She blamed the coffee mug, probably still suffering from her \'93fieldwork\'94 at the office. This was shaping up to be a long, unsatisfying night.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She poured herself some synthetic milk over a glug of tropical rum and got back to her puter, trawling the data swamps.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Unlike Chantara\'92s records, Halo\'92s came up thick and fast: regular health, career, and consumer history. Minor scraps with councils; fines\'97speeding, parking, voting absences; a credible mix of business, charity, VIP club and gym subscriptions. But nothing tying him to the Stickett Inn, not in health, not in purchase records. For a regular, that was odd.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then the address: affluent, harbour-side, a far cry from a Cloudborne local bar. She checked Abe\'92s photos again\'97most shot in the same venue, which had to be the Inn. Why would Halo present as a pub fixture, but live miles away in luxury? There he was, drinking in the epicentre of those Scarlet Shower Surgeon cases.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A fresh line of worry had Hayley dragging herself back to her puter. Instead of bunking off for sleep, she opened the file on Scarlet Shower Surgeon victims: screens of shots, most sourced from families or license records\'97school formals, graduation day, work functions, a few solemn passport-style faces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She dragged Abe\'92s album of Halo\'92s conquests into split view, the snaps in the bar grainy, flecked with hazy Layer light and motion blur. Still, she set to work, flicking from one side to the other, adjusting angles, running facial comparison overlays and, when tech failed, squinting and second-guessing with her own eyes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There. A woman in profile with a dimple by her lower lip. In Halo\'92s photo, she\'92s laughing, half-turned, but that same dimple is there, catching the light.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another. Frizzy red hair, eyes gone wide in flash. Nowhere near as polished as the family photo, but undeniable once spotted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She toggled back and forth. A heavy-lidded smile in an office Christmas party; the very same, caught blurry at Halo\'92s side, arm twisted for a selfie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A tattooed collarbone is just visible in a blurry pub image\'97half seen in a graduation portrait.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With every match, Hayley\'92s stomach knotted tighter. She tried facial recognition aids, then relied on gut, on detail: moles, brow shape, the set of a jaw. By the time she\'92d gone through the folder twice, almost twelve percent of the victims could, with varying confidence, be linked to grainy shots of them with Halo on a night out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley sat back and stared at the evidence, pulse rising, until her throat burned raw. She couldn\'92t even find comfort in the clarity of the victim portraits. Their old, unafraid faces stared straight past her, unknowing. The sinking certainty was new, cold, and unmistakable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was no question: Halo was the connection.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She wondered if she should call someone, if she could trust anyone. Uncharacteristically, Hayley felt very small, very tired, and very much awake.
